WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.097
USING is a shorter way to express an equality join when both tables use the same column name

00:00:07.097 --> 00:00:12.522
for the relationship. In this example, both patients and admissions contain a column named patient ID.

00:00:12.522 --> 00:00:17.007
Instead of writing an ON condition that compares the two columns explicitly,

00:00:17.007 --> 00:00:20.157
PostgreSQL lets us write USING patient ID.

00:00:20.157 --> 00:00:22.142
The relationship is the same.

00:00:22.142 --> 00:00:26.607
We are still matching patients and admissions when the patient IDs are equal.

00:00:26.607 --> 00:00:32.737
One convenience is that the join key appears once in the result rather than as two separate copies.

00:00:32.737 --> 00:00:38.492
USING is helpful when the shared key name is clear and the relationship is a simple equality.

00:00:38.492 --> 00:00:44.782
It is not appropriate when the two tables use different column names, when multiple conditions are needed,

00:00:44.782 --> 00:00:47.392
or when the relationship is more complex.

00:00:47.392 --> 00:00:54.427
Whether you write ON or USING, you should still be able to explain the relationship in plain language.

00:00:54.427 --> 00:00:57.486
Shorter syntax should not replace understanding.
